<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Commune of Reggio</h1><p class="st-meta">1136 CE – 1409 CE (approx.) · capital Reggio Emilia</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Emilian city-republic between Parma and Modena that changed masters many times before passing finally to the Este in 1409.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-emilia">Reggio</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/via-aemilia">Via Emilia</a>, had consuls by 1136, after the citizens took power from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> and from the lords of the Canossa lands. The commune extended its control into the Apennine foothills.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The commune was governed by consuls, then a podestà and popular councils, and joined the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lombard-league">Lombard League</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a>. Its position between larger neighbours made it a pawn: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">Este</a> held it from 1289 to 1306, and in the 14th century it passed among various lords, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gonzaga">Gonzaga</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feltrino-gonzaga">Feltrino Gonzaga</a> and then the Visconti. Factions of the Fogliani, Roberti and Canossa families divided the city.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1409 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-iii-deste">Niccolò III d'Este</a> acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reggio-emilia">Reggio</a>, which then remained an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">Este</a> possession and part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-modena-and-reggio">Duchy of Modena and Reggio</a> until 1859. In 1797 it hosted the congress of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cispadane-republic">Cispadane Republic</a>.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 1136 CE – 1409 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Italy</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:82.31%;width:5.43%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1136 CE – 1409 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Reggio Emilia</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Feltrino Gonzaga</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Emilian, Latin</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Reggio Emilia (commune)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1272&amp;focus=commune-of-reggio" aria-label="Commune of Reggio on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4934 598 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Commune of Reggio lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5254 854L5277 849"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5277 849h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5254 854h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5254 854h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Reggio Emilia. <a href="/atlas?year=1272&amp;focus=commune-of-reggio">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

